hi I'm Nick Gillespie for recent TV  today we're talking to Jeffrey Myron  author of the forthcoming book  libertarianism from A to Z Jeff you  teach at Harvard you're an economist  you've written a book what prompted the  the volume I taught a course about six  seven years ago when I first moved to  Harvard title the libertarian  perspective on economic and social  policy  I came to revisited I said what should I  teach they said whatever you want just  teach your stuff oh wow I wonder what my  stuff is and I sat down and started  thinking about my views on policy very  broadly I thought a lot about drug  policy before that but then I started  just going systematically through  everything and I taught that to the  students they seemed to like it and so I  had these lecture notes and I thought oh  lecture notes are sort of halfway to a  book you have been teaching college for  how many years now about twenty five  years yeah do you is there any sense we  often hear that libertarianism is the  next it philosophy for Generation X Y Z  ABC whatever do you have a sense of  students are they more or less  libertarian than they were 25 years ago  my guess is they're a little bit more  libertarian you definitely do not get  the feeling of the strong social  conservatism amongst very many of the  students but overall I don't think it's  a radical change overall they seem to  roughly reflect the politics with their  parents so there's some some  conservatives or some Republicans  there's some Democrats and there are a  few libertarians maybe a little bit more  tolerant on the social stuff then I  think was true what I want to call are  they more skeptical of government power  and of corporate power than they used to  be or really noisy I think it's noise  around zero I mean I don't see any  obvious change in the overall attitude  let's talk about libertarianism from A  to Z and and your first entry is in a is  abortion  what is how do you how do you deal with  abortion in the book so the position I  outline there is that it's a relatively  subtle hard issue it's not one where I  think libertarians or anyone should take  an extremely strong  position at either extreme if you think  that fetus becomes a person then at some  point sort of laws against murder must  kick in nobody thinks that there should  be abortion for five-year-old kids hey  but you're a parent let's say we we  might rethink that we don't want to take  a hard line against that obviously  parents we think that once in a while  you know obviously the other extreme not  very many people think that a  two-week-old fetus is equivalent to a  human life  so rather than sort of arguing about  where life begins or ends I think we  should need to think about what the  policy what would happen if we had a  policy of banning abortion and one thing  that would happen is some people would  still get abortion so that sort of  awkward it breeds disrespect for the law  or Awards people who are willing to  break the law relative people who don't  and so to me the natural thing is to  define legal abortion somewhere in  between and even better to leave defying  legal abortion to low levels of  government to States rather than to the  federal government why is that better  it's better in my view because there  will be variety there will maybe be a  few states that absolutely ban it but I  don't think very many there will be a  lot of states where it's more or less  identical to current policy quite widely  available legally there'll be a lot of  state serve in between or have some  degree of regulation and I think that  under that variety most people whatever  their views will not feel that the  government has squashed their ideas a  squash their position has disrespected  their strong feelings now you in the  book it's you you talk about  consequential libertarianism or that's  what you are define that a little bit  and explain how that fits because a lot  of people would say well abortion is not  something it's it's you know it's an  inalienable human right or either to  have one or to protect unborn children  from them so how is a consequentialist  libertarian perspective different than  say a rights-based libertarian  perspective I basically mean costs and  benefits I think that rather than saying  there are certain rights we say what  will be the consequences of having this  policy versus that policy in the case of  abortion if we have a policy of banning  it we may reduce the number of abortions  but we won't stop it and we'll have a  lot of absorbing consequences we may  have negative effects for some of the  kids who are born who might have  terminated for some of the families or  other pre-existing kids in families  where those kids might have been born  and I think that when people really  think about it they don't really believe  in these absolute rights so take the  right to be alive  hey most people recognize that taking  another human life is acceptable not  ideal but acceptable in some  circumstances say in self-defense so  people sort of implicitly use this  balancing of cause and benefits and I  think that's what it makes sense to do  broadly and that's where I come out on  abortion the final entry is for Z azuz  what up what about series what's the  consequentialist libertarian take on  zoos the take on zoos is zoos are fine  but government support for zoos is  doesn't have any very good good  justification and means more taxes it  means that some government decides  whether there should be a big zoo a  small zoo whether it should be a zoo as  opposed to an orchestra and so  government shouldn't be supporting arts  and culture more generally they should  be letting individuals choose what books  what movies what music etc their  interests yet the origin of zoos are the  kind of modern origin of zoos really is  a European 17th century 16th century of  royal leaders having expeditions come  back with fancy animals so the Royal  menagerie has always been a state  institution so what let me ask you this  what's the chance of getting rid of zoos  of finally saying to the Columbus Zoo  the San Diego Zoo the National Zoo you  know you're on your own you know that  one I actually think we have a chance  because states and cities are facing  these huge budgetary pressures it might  not be so hard to convince yourself as a  city council or whatever gee if we sell  the zoo off to some private entrepreneur  who will run it on a somewhat more  efficient basis we'll get some money the  zoo will probably still be there and so  maybe that's the way to go there in fact  was an article in the paper the other  day about selling off state liquor  stores right now of course if the states  had been running them in a profit  maximizing way they wouldn't gain  anything by selling them off but states  don't do things here who else oh they  probably will gain by selling them off  let me throw in one final entry that's  also a difficult one one Civil Rights  Act of 1964 what is why is that in the  book I put that in the book because  as a lot of people including some  libertarians think that the government  should play some role in preventing  discrimination or in punishing people  who discriminate on the basis of race  sex etc and in particular a lot of  people would point to the Civil Rights  Act as having changed the situation for  blacks relative to whites in the u.s.  quite dramatically and I think that  that's too strong because I think that  there were two things going on one was  that a number of federal actions  prevented state governments from  actively engaging in discriminatory  policies okay explicit Jim Crow laws and  that part I think actually makes total  sense it's consistent with  libertarianism consistent with the  Constitution many more other things that  are done under the name of the Civil  Rights Act such as affirmative action  imposed by government and by affirmative  action you mean quotas based on the  course not simply saying that you know  we should make an attempt to advertise  jobs in among publications that  underserved historically and represented  minorities might be part of right  affirmative action means a broad range  of things and some seem sort of quite  innocuous that are probably not very  costly but affirmative action seems to  evolve if it's going to have teeth into  quotas and I think those are sort of  unfortunate of an umber of reasons  namely because they lead to undervaluing  of the accomplishments of minorities who  would succeed on their own they lead to  stereotypes that minorities can't  succeed on them by themselves I think  that's counterproductive for a longer  term move toward an egalitarian society  you know with the Civil Rights Act of  1964 which I guess is within libertarian  circles is most controversial for really  redefining a public space and a private  space is it I mean how do you do a  cost-benefit analysis of saying you know  what there's a real value to legally  redefining a hotel or a movie theater or  something as a public accommodation  which cannot discriminate because it  would seem that that's actually a very  you know there's a lot of clear  beneficial consequences to that and what  is exactly what gets lost there and I  guess I don't see huge beneficial  consequences because I think if private  if hotels if restaurants etc  discriminated you know even there were  no law they  would lose business they would be under  commercial pressure profit pressure to  stop doing that or other entrepreneurs  would come in and serve the groups that  were not being served so I don't think  there's such a big benefit and I think  there's a slippery slope that the more  we say government has the right to  intervene for some public good the more  we set the stage for government to  define a whole huge range of things as  being for the public good well thank you  very much I hope the book does well and  I certainly hope the philosophy does for  a reason TV I'm Nick Gillespie and we  were talking with Jeff Myron author of  the forthcoming book libertarianism from  A to Z
 

